{0 Getting Started}

This guide walks you through installing Hedgehog and writing your first
property test.

{1 Installation}

Install via opam:

{@bash skip[
opam install hedgehog
]}

{1 Project setup}

Add Hedgehog to your [dune-project]:

{@dune[
(lang dune 3.0)

(package
 (name my-project)
 (depends
  (hedgehog (>= 0.1))))
]}

And to your test executable's [dune] file:

{@dune[
(test
 (name my_tests)
 (libraries hedgehog))
]}

{1 Your first property}

Create [test/my_tests.ml]:

{@ocaml[
open Hedgehog

let prop_reverse_reverse =
  Property.(property Gen.(
    let* xs = list (Range.linear 0 100) (int (Range.linear 0 1000)) in
    return (fun () ->
      assert_ (List.rev (List.rev xs) = xs))))

let () =
  let passed =
    Property.check_group
      { name = "my first tests"
      ; properties =
          [ "reverse reverse", prop_reverse_reverse
          ]
      }
  in
  if not passed then exit 1
]}

{1 Running tests}

{@bash skip[
dune runtest
]}

You should see output like:

{v
━━━ my first tests ━━━
  ✓ reverse reverse passed 100 tests.

  ✓ 1 succeeded.
v}

{1 A failing property}

Let's write a property that will fail, to see how Hedgehog reports
counterexamples with shrinking:

{@ocaml[
open Hedgehog

let show_list xs = "[" ^ String.concat "; " (List.map string_of_int xs) ^ "]"

let prop_bad_sort =
  Property.(property Gen.(
    let* xs = list (Range.linear 0 100) (int (Range.linear 0 1000)) in
    return (fun () ->
      annotate ("xs = " ^ show_list xs);
      let sorted = List.sort Int.compare xs in
      assert_ (sorted = xs))))

let () =
  Property.check prop_bad_sort |> ignore
]}

Integrated shrinking reduces the failure to a minimal counterexample
without any extra work from you — a two-element list is the smallest
list that is not already sorted:

{v
*** Failed! Falsifiable (after 4 tests and 62 shrinks):
  xs = [1; 0]
  Assertion failed
v}

Note the [annotate] call. [assert_] reports only that the assertion
failed, so without an annotation the report has no counterexample in it
at all. For structured values, {!page-"tutorial"} shows how [diff]
prints the counterexample as a line-level diff instead:

{@ocaml[
open Hedgehog

let show_lines xs = String.concat "\n" (List.map string_of_int xs)

let () =
  Property.check
    Property.(property Gen.(
      let* xs = list (Range.linear 0 100) (int (Range.linear 0 1000)) in
      return (fun () ->
        diff show_lines ( = ) show_lines (List.sort Int.compare xs) xs)))
  |> ignore
]}

{v
*** Failed! Falsifiable (after 4 tests and 43 shrinks):
  - 0
    1
  + 0
v}

The sorted list is on the [-] side and the generated one on the [+]
side: [0] moved from the front to the back, and the [1] they share is
kept as context.

